            <ArticleTitle>360 degrees or 180 degrees for bone SPECT of the spine?</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>279-82</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Acquisitions of 360 degrees are usually performed for bone single photon emission computed tomography (SPECT). However, the spine is a peripheral and median structure, it therefore seemed interesting to compare the 360 degrees and the posterior 180 degrees SPECT images. In a phantom study and in 25 adult patients, the 360 degrees SPECT images were compared with the posterior 180 degrees images obtained during the same acquisition: the posterior 180 degrees images are at least equivalent to the 360 degrees images. With a reduction in the examination time, SPECT could become easier to perform, especially in patients suffering from back pain.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
